    "decision": "Keep the logical Prefill/Decode split and measurement hooks, then optimize phase-local kernels before building a general double/triple-buffer or Prefill/Decode-overlap scheduler.",
    "evidence": [
      "The single-request execution is still dependency-serialized; a generated token cannot overlap its own Prefill or prior Decode step.",
      "GPU span idle is only 0.84% to 2.03% across the four traces, so generic same-request buffering has little directly exposed idle time to recover.",
      "P19 with 25 Decode steps spends 2.780 s raw kernel time in Decode, led by NVFP4 gate/up, FP8 projections, and NVFP4 down.",
      "P513 Prefill is dominated by NVFP4 gate/up/down and attention scores; attention-score raw time alone is 24.08% of prefix raw time.",
      "The existing M32 auxiliary-stream overlap is measurable, but event tracing perturbs the one-sample timing and does not justify deleting synchronization edges."
    ],
    "ordered_next_work": [
      "Use the frozen phase baseline to screen bounded Prefill kernels, prioritizing long-context attention scores and the repeated NVFP4 projection path.",
      "Use the P19 Decode profile to screen M1 NVFP4/FP8 projection improvements independently, with exact semantic and mirrored end-to-end gates.",
      "Only after phase-local wins, design multi-request continuous batching or a phase scheduler where independent requests can overlap; define KV-memory, fairness, TTFT, inter-token-latency, and tail-latency gates first.",
      "Treat double/triple buffering as a kernel- or pipeline-specific mechanism, not as a blanket architecture milestone."
    ]
